{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794835778,"nonce":"mzTUCQRzXSPzlYjzYCO7wGnit0Y=","message":"Variance of CPU at rest with spinners in view (user saw 7-24%), measured 2026-11-16 on cn_progress_window SPINNERS=4 and cn_demo, BLINC_TRACE_PROBE=1.\n\nTwo separate sources:\n\n1. Per-frame GPU-API time varied 4-8x between fast and slow frames (dynamic_batch 196 -\u003e 831 us, composite_frame 119 -\u003e 684 us, bindings 33 -\u003e 272 us) while a fixed CPU calibration loop varied 33 -\u003e 43 us. `sample` showed the time in `Queue::write_buffer`: wgpu-core allocates a staging buffer per call (IOGPUResourceCreate, a mach_msg into the kernel) and frees it at the next submit's maintain (another kernel trap). Six writes, ~3.8 KB, per frame at rest; kernel round-trips vary with GPU-driver contention. Also `set_glyph_atlas` rebuilt the SDF bind group on every call (2 per frame).\n\n   Change (uncommitted at time of writing): renderer writes go through a reused StagingBelt (`renderer/uploads.rs`), copies recorded in a command buffer placed ahead of the next submit, so `write_buffer` ordering holds; `queue()`/`queue_arc()` flush pending uploads first. `set_glyph_atlas` keeps clones of the bound views and skips an unchanged rebind (views compare by resource, so atlas growth still rebinds).\n\n   Main-thread render samples over 5 s: 232 -\u003e 41. Stage medians (noisy, CPU clock varies 2x between runs): composite_frame 276 -\u003e 87 us, dynamic_batch 353 -\u003e 118 us.\n\n2. Process CPU is proportional to frame rate, and frame rate is not constant: 40-97 fps per 5 s window with fps_cap 120 on a ProMotion display, dt mostly 9-13 ms. CPU per frame stayed at 2.0-2.4 ms (whole process, trace on) while CPU% moved with fps. Of that, the main thread is about half; four Metal/CA worker threads take the rest (CommandQueueDispatch submit, CompletionQueueDispatch, FramePacing LayerStateSync), which scale with command buffers per frame. A frame at rest makes 2 submits, each with an upload command buffer when there are writes.\n\nNext: one submit per frame (the \"single submit\" item above) cuts the worker-thread share; then find why the loop paces at ~90 fps with jitter rather than at the display rate.","files":null}]}